# §128 — augmented triangles sharpen global defect payment

Use canonical Section-123 augmented cell graphs `J_r^+`. Let `T_r` be their bounded
triangular-face counts, `Q_r^+` their augmented degree excesses, and

\[
T:=\sum_rT_r,
\qquad
Q^+:=\sum_rQ_r^+.
\]

Let `m` be the number of nonunit sides of the loaded force hull, `Q` the global bounded
face-degree excess, and `u_B` the canonical outer-walk surplus.

For sufficiently large Type-II survivors,

\[
\boxed{T\le m}
\tag{128.1}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
Q^+
=Q+u_B-(m-T)-2(c_I-1)-d_{tile}.
}
\tag{128.2}
\]

Consequently

\[
\boxed{Q^+\le Q+u_B}
\tag{128.3}
\]

and canonical Section 123 improves from the factor-10 coarse payment to

\[
\boxed{
\sum_rP_{{\rm exc},r}
\le5(Q+u_B)
=5(e_{BB}-2k).
}
\tag{128.4}
\]

## 128.1 Geometry of one augmented triangle

A bounded triangular face of `J_r^+` must use an added side of the attachment polygon,
because the old contact graph is triangle-free. It cannot use two added polygon sides:
if `|A_r|>3`, the old third side would be a unit contact between globally
nonconsecutive loaded vertices, contradicting the audited Type-II `c_B=0` theorem; if
`|A_r|=3`, the triangle would be the entire attachment polygon boundary and could not
be a bounded face because `I_r` is nonempty and strictly interior.

Hence every augmented triangle has vertices

\[
a-x-b,
\qquad a,b\in A_r,
\qquad x\in I_r,
\]

with

\[
|a-x|=|x-b|=1,
\qquad
1<|a-b|\le2,
\]

and `ab` is the unique added noncontact polygon side.

The apex `x` cannot be loaded. If it were, both unit sides would be global hull sides.
For `ab` to remain a side of the attachment polygon, the cyclic attachment set would
then have to be exactly `{a,x,b}`, making the triangle the outer polygon boundary
rather than a bounded face.

## 128.2 The base is a nonunit global hull side

Suppose the base `ab`, with `|a-b|<=2`, were not a global hull side. The Type-II
near-disk normal-cone estimate puts all sides of the short polar hull arc from `a` to
`b` in an `o(1)` tangent cone. Projection along the cone midpoint excludes three or
more hull sides on this arc; hence exactly one intermediate loaded vertex `y` remains.
Let

\[
s=|a-b|,
\qquad
\ell_1=|a-y|,
\qquad
\ell_2=|y-b|.
\]

Unit separation plus the projection estimate forces

\[
s\to2,
\qquad
\ell_1,\ell_2\to1.
\]

For `M=(a+b)/2`, the tooth apex satisfies

\[
|x-M|^2=1-s^2/4\to0,
\]

while the exact median identity gives

\[
|y-M|^2
={\ell_1^2+\ell_2^2\over2}-{s^2\over4}
\to0.
\]

Thus `|x-y|->0`, contradicting source unit separation. Hence `ab` is a nonunit global
hull side.

The charge from augmented triangles to global base sides is injective: a polygon side
borders at most one bounded face inside one cell, and two positive-area cells cannot
share the same global hull side while both lie in its inward half-plane and have
disjoint interiors. This proves (128.1).

## 128.3 Exact algebra

Canonical Section 123 gives

\[
e_{BB}-k={1\over2}(a-T+Q^+)+d_{tile}.
\]

The centralized Type-II hull-side theorem gives

\[
c_B=0,
\qquad
e_{BB}=q-m=Q+2k+u_B.
\]

Also

\[
a=q-2+2c_I-d_{tile}.
\]

Eliminating `a` and `e_BB` yields (128.2). For large Type II, `c_I>=1`, while
`m-T>=0` and `d_tile>=0`; hence (128.3). Canonical Section 123 has
`P_{{\rm exc},r}<=5Q_r^+`, proving (128.4).

The corresponding necessary Type-II payment is

\[
\liminf {1\over D}
\left[
\sum_r(L_r-2d_r)+5(Q+u_B)
\right]
\ge {\pi\over2}(2-\sqrt3).
\tag{128.5}
\]

