# §129 — full-core strict-rhombus localization

Let `R_4` be the number of bounded strict-rhombus faces of the full nonisolated
positive-contact core and `R_r` the number of bounded quadrilateral faces in harmonic
cell `H_r`. Then, for sufficiently large Type-II survivors,

\[
\boxed{
0\le R_4-\sum_rR_r\le3c_I+5=o(D).
}
\tag{129.1}
\]

If `A_4` is total full-core strict-rhombus area and `A_{4,r}` the cell areas, then

\[
\boxed{
0\le A_4-\sum_rA_{4,r}=o(D).
}
\tag{129.2}
\]

The area bound uses the correct universal estimate

\[
\operatorname{area}(\text{unit rhombus})\le1.
\]

The historical sentence `area<=sqrt(3)/2` was false; a unit square is the counterexample.

## 129.1 Local quadrilateral fidelity

A local quadrilateral face is a strict unit rhombus. Its diagonals are strictly larger
than one—equality would make a unit equilateral triangle with adjacent unit sides—and
the squared diagonals sum to four, so every half-diagonal is strictly less than one.
Splitting at the center shows any omitted source point in the rhombus interior would be
strictly less than one from a rhombus-side endpoint. Unit separation excludes this;
straight-line contact planarity excludes an omitted crossing edge. Thus each local
quadrilateral is a physical full-core rhombus face.

No physical rhombus is counted by two harmonic cells: an unloaded vertex identifies its
component, while an all-loaded duplicate would give two components at least three
common attachments, forbidden by canonical Section 83.

## 129.2 Missing-rhombus classification

Classify a full-core rhombus not realized locally by its number of loaded vertices.

- **0 or 1 loaded:** the unloaded contact path lies in one component and every loaded
  cycle vertex attaches to it, so the face is local.
- **2 adjacent loaded:** the two unloaded vertices are contact-adjacent and hence in one
  component; again local.
- **2 opposite loaded:** a missing face requires two distinct unloaded components
  sharing the same opposite loaded pair. Their convex cells share the loaded diagonal
  as a common side and lie on opposite sides. Missing faces inject into cell-side
  adjacencies; the cell adjacency graph is planar, giving at most `3c_I`.
- **3 loaded:** if the middle loaded vertex attached to the unloaded component, the
  face would be local. Otherwise its two incident unit contacts are global hull sides.
  The strict-rhombus angle at that hull vertex lies in `(pi/3,2pi/3)`, so exterior hull
  turn is `>pi/3`. Total turn `2pi` permits at most five such vertices/faces.
- **4 loaded:** every rhombus side is a loaded unit contact and therefore a global hull
  side. Four such sides would close the whole loaded hull, forcing `q=4`, impossible
  in large Type II.

This proves (129.1); (129.2) follows from area `<=1` per missing rhombus.

