# §130 — augmented triangles are exactly two-edge outer teeth

Write loaded hull vertices cyclically as `(b_1,...,b_q)`, let

\[
s_i=|b_i-b_{i+1}|,
\]

and let `W_i` be the canonical outer-face interval from `b_i` to `b_{i+1}`, with
occurrence length `ell_i`. Put

\[
M:=\{i:s_i>1\},
\qquad m:=|M|.
\]

Canonical Section 63 has

\[
x_B=\sum_i(\ell_i-1),
\qquad
u_B:=x_B-m\ge0.
\]

Let `T=sum_rT_r` be the augmented triangular-face count. Then

\[
\boxed{
T=\#\{i\in M:\ell_i=2\}.
}
\tag{130.1}
\]

Consequently

\[
\boxed{0\le m-T\le u_B.}
\tag{130.2}
\]

## 130.1 Two-edge nonunit interval gives one triangle

If `i in M` and `ell_i=2`, write the outer interval

\[
b_i-x-b_{i+1}.
\]

The middle point is unloaded: if it were loaded, both unit contacts would be global
hull sides, incompatible with `b_i,b_{i+1}` already being consecutive hull vertices
for large `q>3`.

Let `I_r` contain `x`. Then `b_i,b_{i+1}` are consecutive attachments, so their
nonunit chord is an added side of `P_r`. Adding the missing global hull side cuts from
the old outer face exactly the triangular pocket

\[
b_i-x-b_{i+1}-b_i.
\]

The pocket contains no positive-core vertex. Section 83 excludes foreign unloaded
components, loaded vertices are extreme points, and the old outer-face pocket itself
contains no embedded core edge. Hence the same triangle is a bounded face of `J_r^+`.

## 130.2 Every augmented triangle is such a tooth

By §128, an augmented triangle has unloaded apex `x` and nonunit global hull base
`ab`. Its open interior contains no full-core vertex or edge. After adding `ab` to the
global hull augmentation it is exactly the inward face incident to `ab`; removing the
added side shows the distinguished old outer interval between `a,b` is the two-edge
path `a-x-b`. Global base-side injection makes this correspondence one-to-one, proving
(130.1).

Finally

\[
\boxed{
 u_B
 =\sum_{i\in M}(\ell_i-2)
 +\sum_{i\notin M}(\ell_i-1).
}
\tag{130.3}
\]

Every nonunit index not counted by `T` has `ell_i>=3` and therefore contributes at
least one to the first sum. This proves (130.2).

Putting

\[
r_{deep}:=m-T,
\]

§128 becomes

\[
\boxed{
Q^+=Q+(u_B-r_{deep})-2(c_I-1)-d_{tile}.
}
\tag{130.4}
\]

