# §131 — harmonic-face fidelity and sharp global defect injection

For harmonic cell `r`, let

\[
P_r=\operatorname{conv}(A_r)
\]

and `H_r` be the induced positive-contact graph on `I_r union A_r`. Canonical Section
82 gives `I_r subset int(P_r)` and convex local bounded faces; Section 83 gives
pairwise disjoint cell interiors.

Then

\[
\boxed{V(G)\cap\operatorname{int}P_r=I_r.}
\tag{131.1}
\]

A foreign unloaded vertex lies in another cell interior and is excluded by Section 83.
A loaded vertex outside `A_r` is an extreme point of the force hull and cannot lie in
the convex hull of the other loaded vertices containing `P_r`.

Let `C` be a bounded face of `H_r`. Its open interior contains no full-core vertex.
An omitted straight contact edge cannot enter and leave `C` without either properly
crossing a boundary contact or joining two boundary vertices; the latter edge would
already belong to the induced graph `H_r`. Hence `C` is an actual bounded face of the
full positive-contact core.

The same global bounded face cannot occur in two harmonic cells because its nonempty
open interior would lie in two cell interiors. Thus local bounded faces inject into
global bounded faces with degree preserved.

Let `f,Q` be the global bounded nonquadrilateral face count and degree excess. Then

\[
\boxed{
\sum_rP_{{\rm exc},r}\le4f+Q.
}
\tag{131.2}
\]

Since `f<=Q` and canonical Section 63 has

\[
e_{BB}=Q+2k+u_B+c_B,
\]

we obtain

\[
\boxed{
\sum_rP_{{\rm exc},r}
\le4f+Q
\le5Q
\le5(e_{BB}-k).
}
\tag{131.3}
\]

This is a finite face-identification theorem; contact-isolated full-source points may
lie in a face interior without splitting the positive-contact core face.

