# §132 — no strict-rhombus train returns to one bounded face

Equip the post-ZF positive-contact core with a relative-interior KKT stress, so every
positive contact has multiplier `alpha_e>0`. Let `B=Vert(F)` be the loaded force-hull
vertices.

Assume a nontrivial stopped train has both terminal sides on one bounded global face.
Close its simple midpoint path by an arc through that open face. The resulting Jordan
curve lies in the force hull and crosses the contact graph exactly at the parallel train
contacts.

Its bounded Jordan domain contains no loaded vertex: loaded vertices are extreme
points of the force hull, whereas a point in the open Jordan domain has a planar
neighborhood contained in the force hull. Hence no active diameter edge crosses the
bounded shore.

Orient the common train-edge direction `u` so every cut contact points into the bounded
shore. The contact resultant is

\[
\left(\sum_{e\in E(\tau)}\alpha_e\right)u\ne0.
\]

Summing KKT equilibrium over the bounded shore cancels all internal contact/diameter
forces and leaves no crossing diameter force, so the contact resultant would have to
vanish. Contradiction.

Thus

\[
\boxed{
\text{no nontrivial stopped strict-rhombus train returns to one bounded face.}
}
\tag{132.1}
\]

Using §131, the same is true for one bounded harmonic-cell defect face. Outer-to-outer,
outer-to-bounded, and distinct-bounded-face trains remain possible.

