# §133 — macroscopic inactive corridors are phase-pure endpoint hosts

Let `C` be a convex unit-edge polygon of degree `n_C`; choose farthest boundary
vertices `a,b`, put

\[
r:=|a-b|=\operatorname{diam}C,
\qquad q:={b-a\over r}.
\]

For `0<eta<=pi/2`, let `N_C(eta)` count boundary edge occurrences whose projective
direction is at least `eta` from `[q]`. Then

\[
\boxed{
N_C(\eta)
\le {n_C-2r\over1-\cos\eta}.
}
\tag{133.1}
\]

and, for either orientation `u_e` of each projective edge direction,

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{e\subset\partial C}(1-|q\cdot u_e|)
\le n_C-2r.
}
\tag{133.2}
\]

Indeed, orient each of the two boundary arcs from `a` to `b`. For any directed unit-edge
route of length `m` and displacement `rq`,

\[
m-r=\sum_i(1-q\cdot v_i).
\]

A projectively `eta`-bad edge costs at least `1-cos eta`. The two arcs have disjoint
occurrence sets and total excess `n_C-2r`, proving (133.1). Since
`q·v_e<=|q·u_e|`, the same sum proves (133.2).

In Type II, canonical Section 84 gives for every linear-degree bounded inactive face

\[
n_C-2\operatorname{diam}C=o(D).
\]

Hence, for every fixed `eta>0`, only `o(D)` boundary occurrences are transverse by
`eta` to the corridor direction, and the total weighted alignment defect is `o(D)`.

By §131 this applies to bounded harmonic-cell defect faces with physical occurrences
preserved; by §132, a train cannot use the same bounded face at both ends. Therefore a
linear endpoint population on one macroscopic bounded face consists of linearly many
distinct trains, all but `o(D)` in one projective phase window.

Cutting the face at its diameter pair adds hosts `q,-q`; the same physical corridor
excess pays the endpoint alignment defect. Thus a macroscopic corridor cannot be
introduced as a directionally arbitrary free signed-return host.

