# §141 — consecutive tooth turn budget, with binding ray-order repair

Let `p_i,p_{i+1},p_{i+2}` be consecutive vertices of the loaded hull. Suppose the two
adjacent hull sides are nonunit tooth bases

\[
b_i=1+e_i,
\qquad
b_{i+1}=1+e_{i+1},
\qquad
0<e_i,e_{i+1}<1,
\]

with unloaded unit-tooth apexes `x_i,x_{i+1}`. Let `alpha_i` be the exterior hull turn
at `p_{i+1}` and define

\[
\beta(e):=\arccos{1+e\over2},
\qquad
f(e):={\pi\over3}-\beta(e).
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{
\alpha_i\le f(e_i)+f(e_{i+1}).
}
\tag{141.1}
\]

## 141.1 Correct ray order

Direct the preceding hull side at argument zero. The ray from `p_{i+1}` to the first
apex has argument

\[
\theta_i=\pi-\beta(e_i),
\]

while the ray to the next apex has argument

\[
\theta_{i+1}=\alpha_i+\beta(e_{i+1}).
\]

The historical proof incorrectly said their common inward wedge alone fixes their
order. The correct argument also uses unit separation from the first apex to the next
loaded hull vertex `p_{i+2}`.

If `theta_{i+1}>theta_i`, then

\[
0\le\theta_i-\alpha_i<\beta(e_{i+1}).
\]

Because

\[
|x_i-p_{i+1}|=1,
\qquad
|p_{i+2}-p_{i+1}|=b_{i+1},
\]

the cosine law gives

\[
|x_i-p_{i+2}|^2
=1+b_{i+1}^2
-2b_{i+1}\cos(\theta_i-\alpha_i)
<1,
\]

since `cos beta(e_{i+1})=b_{i+1}/2`. This violates source unit separation. Hence

\[
\theta_{i+1}\le\theta_i.
\]

The angular separation of the two unit apex rays is therefore

\[
\gamma_i
=\pi-\alpha_i-\beta(e_i)-\beta(e_{i+1}).
\]

Unit separation of `x_i,x_{i+1}` gives

\[
1\le2\sin(\gamma_i/2),
\]

so `gamma_i>=pi/3`, proving (141.1).

## 141.2 Tooth-run consequences

For a consecutive run of tooth sides `r,...,s`,

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{i=r}^{s-1}\alpha_i
\le
f(e_r)+2\sum_{i=r+1}^{s-1}f(e_i)+f(e_s).
}
\tag{141.2}
\]

If every side of a convex loaded cycle is a tooth, summing cyclically gives

\[
\sum_i f(e_i)\ge\pi.
\tag{141.3}
\]

The function `f` is increasing and strictly convex, with `f(0)=0`, `f(1)=pi/3`.
Convexity gives

\[
f(e)\le{\pi\over3}e,
\]

hence the universal finite bound

\[
\boxed{\sum_i(b_i-1)\ge3.}
\tag{141.4}
\]

If every `e_i<=eta<1`, then

\[
f'(e)={1\over\sqrt{4-(1+e)^2}}
\le {1\over\sqrt{4-(1+\eta)^2}},
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
\sum_i(b_i-1)
\ge\pi\sqrt{4-(1+\eta)^2}.
}
\tag{141.5}
\]

As `eta->0`, the right side tends to `pi sqrt(3)`. This remains only an `O(1)` total
base-excess cost for a `Theta(D)` tooth cycle and therefore does not close C-II.

